$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General/Typical Procedure: General Procedure for Oxidation of Terminal
Olefins. To a stirred solution of olefin (0.5 mmol) in CH3CN (3.5 mL) and
H2O (0.5 mL) were added PdCl2 (4.4 mg, 0.025 mmol, 5 mol %) and
Fe2(SO4)3·nH2O (600 mg, 1.5 mmol, 1.5 equiv) at room temperature. The
reaction mixture was warmed to 45 °C and stirred for a specified time
(see Tables 2 and 3) under N2. The reaction mixture was then filtered
through a small pad of silica gel and washed with EtOAc, and the filtrate
was concentrated. The residue in some cases contained virtually pure
compound, and no further purification was necessary (for compounds 2a−c
and 2f−h). In other cases the residue was purified by silica gel column
chromatography using petroleum ether/ EtOAc as an eluent to afford the
methyl ketones. Decane-2,9-dione (2d):21a Yield (63 mg, 74%). Colorless
oil. IR (CHCl3): νmax = 3019, 2933, 2858, 1717, 1409, 1363, 1168, 1048,
967, 927, 667 cm−1; 1H NMR (400 MHz, CDCl3/TMS): δ = 2.41 (t, J = 7.4 Hz,
4H), 2.12 (s, 6H), 1.61−1.52 (m, 4H), 1.31−1.25 (m, 4H); 13C {1H} NMR
(100 MHz, CDCl3): δ = 209.2, 43.6, 29.9, 28.9, 23.5; HRMS (ESI-TOF) calcd
for [C10H18O2 + Na]+ 193.1199, found 193.1198.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Iron(III) Sulfate as Terminal Oxidant in the Synthesis of Methyl Ketones
via Wacker Oxidation
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Fernandes, Rodney A.; Chaudhari, Dipali A.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Journal of Organic Chemistry (2014), 79(12), 5787-5793

